text:
"I do not see what good I can do him, William;
and it's not an hour at which I ever receive people.
I am sure I don't know what he can want with me."

"Your sister does not like me," she said to Harry;
"never mind, she is young, and she will know better
one day."

Edward Vernon listened with great edification;
he forgot the flower which he was going to search
for.

"It is true—she does not understand you somehow;
things seem to go the wrong way between
times."

"Did you wish me to pay you compliments? No;
we are arguing out the general question," said
Hester, with her serious face.

"That is nonsense, however," said the old man;
"for of course you must meet. We have got our
grandson, Hester."

"Your arguments are unanswerable," she said;
"there is nothing to be said against such admirable
logic."

"I prefer to be here," Hester answered, shortly;
"never mind me, please. Mr. Rule, will you answer
me? I want to know."

"No; this is dulness—this is nothing," she said;
"not living at all, but only going on because one
cannot help it."

"Nothing of the sort," said Edward, smiling cheerfully;
"the kindest feeling. I know it is. Are you
going to see Harry? He is in his room, I know."
